      "notes": "\n  <h2>Condensation</h2>\n\n  <h3>Meaning of Condensation</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    <strong>Condensation</strong> is the change of state in which a gas\n    changes into a liquid. It occurs when the gas loses thermal energy\n    to its surroundings under suitable conditions.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    During condensation, the particles lose kinetic energy on average.\n    Their movement becomes less vigorous, allowing attractive forces\n    between the particles to bring them closer together in the liquid state.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    The substance remains the same during condensation. Only its physical\n    state changes. For example, water vapour condenses to form liquid water.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>How Condensation Occurs</h3>\n\n  <ol>\n    <li>A substance is present as a gas.</li>\n    <li>The gas transfers thermal energy to its surroundings.</li>\n    <li>The particles lose kinetic energy on average.</li>\n    <li>Under suitable conditions, the particles come closer together.</li>\n    <li>The gas changes into a liquid.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 1: Water Droplets on a Cold Glass</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> A cold glass of water develops droplets\n  on its outside, even though no water has been poured onto the outside.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the source of the water.</p>\n  <p>\n    Water vapour is present in the air surrounding the glass.\n  </p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify what happens to the water vapour.</p>\n  <p>\n    The water vapour transfers thermal energy to the cold glass and\n    the nearby surroundings.\n  </p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the state change.</p>\n  <p>\n    The water vapour changes from a gas into liquid water droplets.\n  </p>\n\n  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The droplets form through condensation\n  of water vapour from the air, not because water passes through the glass.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 2: Droplets on a Bathroom Mirror</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> A mirror becomes covered with tiny\n  water droplets after a hot shower.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Warm air in the bathroom contains water vapour.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The mirror is cooler than the warm, moist air.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Water vapour loses thermal energy near the\n  cooler surface and changes into liquid water.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The droplets on the mirror are formed\n  by condensation.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 3: Formation of Clouds</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> Tiny water droplets form in parts of\n  the atmosphere.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water is present in the atmosphere as water vapour.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> When moist air cools sufficiently, water vapour\n  can condense.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The vapour changes into tiny liquid water\n  droplets that can form clouds.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Condensation changes atmospheric water\n  vapour into liquid droplets. Cloud formation can involve additional\n  atmospheric processes, but the state change described here is condensation.</p>\n\n  <h3>Important Points</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Condensation is a change from gas to liquid.</li>\n    <li>The gas loses thermal energy to its surroundings.</li>\n    <li>The particles lose kinetic energy on average and move closer together.</li>\n    <li>Condensation does not produce a new substance.</li>\n    <li>A cool surface can encourage condensation when water vapour\n    comes into contact with it.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>\n\n  <p>Answer each question in your own words. Explain your reasoning,\n  not just the name of the process.</p>\n  ",
