      "notes": "\n  <h2>Graphite: Structure and Properties</h2>\n\n  <h3>1. Graphite is another form of carbon</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    Graphite and diamond are both forms of the element carbon, but\n    their atoms are arranged differently. This difference in structure\n    gives them different physical properties.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    Graphite has a <b>giant covalent structure made of layers</b>.\n    Each layer contains carbon atoms bonded together in a repeating\n    network of hexagonal rings.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>2. How are carbon atoms bonded in graphite?</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    Each carbon atom forms three strong covalent bonds with three\n    neighbouring carbon atoms in the same layer.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    Carbon has four outer-shell electrons. Three are used in the\n    three covalent bonds. The remaining electron contributes to a\n    system of <b>delocalised electrons</b> within the layer.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    Delocalised electrons are not confined to one particular\n    covalent bond. They can move through the graphite layers and\n    carry electrical charge.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>3. Why does graphite conduct electricity?</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    Graphite conducts electricity because it contains delocalised\n    electrons that can move along its layers.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    When a potential difference is applied, these mobile electrons\n    can carry charge through the material. This makes graphite\n    different from diamond, in which all four outer-shell electrons\n    of each carbon atom are used in covalent bonding.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>4. Why is graphite soft and slippery?</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    The carbon atoms within each layer are connected by strong\n    covalent bonds. However, the attractions between neighbouring\n    layers are much weaker than the covalent bonds within a layer.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    The layers can slide over one another relatively easily.\n    This makes graphite soft and allows it to act as a lubricant\n    in suitable applications.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    Do not say that the covalent bonds inside each layer are weak.\n    The weak attractions are <b>between the layers</b>, not within them.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>5. Why does graphite have a high melting point?</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    Graphite is a giant covalent structure. Strong covalent bonds\n    connect the carbon atoms throughout each layer. A very large\n    amount of energy is needed to disrupt this network.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    Therefore, graphite has a very high melting or sublimation\n    temperature under appropriate conditions. The weak attractions\n    between layers help explain its softness, but they do not mean\n    the entire structure melts at a low temperature.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>6. Uses of graphite</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>\n      <b>Electrodes:</b> graphite conducts electricity and can be\n      used as an electrode in suitable electrochemical processes.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      <b>Lubricants:</b> its layers slide over one another, reducing\n      friction in suitable conditions.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      <b>Pencil cores:</b> layers of graphite rub off onto paper\n      during writing.\n    </li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 1: Explain why graphite conducts electricity</h3>\n\n  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why graphite conducts electricity but\n  diamond does not.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In graphite, each carbon atom forms three covalent\n  bonds with neighbouring atoms.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The remaining outer-shell electron contributes to\n  the delocalised electron system.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Step 3:</b> These electrons can move along the layers and carry\n  electrical charge. In diamond, each carbon uses all four outer-shell\n  electrons in covalent bonds.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Graphite conducts electricity because it has\n  mobile delocalised electrons, whereas diamond has no freely moving\n  electrons under ordinary conditions.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 2: Explain why graphite is soft</h3>\n\n  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why graphite can be used as a lubricant.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Graphite is made of layers of carbon atoms.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Strong covalent bonds hold the atoms together\n  within each layer.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Much weaker attractions act between neighbouring\n  layers, allowing them to slide over one another.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Graphite can act as a lubricant because its\n  layers slide over one another relatively easily.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain the high melting point of graphite</h3>\n\n  <p><b>Question:</b> If the layers in graphite slide easily, why does\n  graphite still have a very high melting temperature?</p>\n\n  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sliding layers does not require breaking all the\n  covalent bonds within those layers.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each layer contains a continuous network of carbon\n  atoms joined by strong covalent bonds.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Disrupting this giant covalent network requires a\n  large amount of energy.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Graphite has a very high melting or sublimation\n  temperature because of its strong covalent bonding within the layers,\n  even though the layers can slide over one another.</p>\n\n  <h3>Common mistake</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    Graphite is not soft because the bonds between carbon atoms\n    within a layer are weak. Those covalent bonds are strong.\n    Its softness results from the relatively weak attractions\n    between the layers.\n  </p>\n  ",
