      "notes": "\n  <h2>Naming Covalent Compounds</h2>\n\n  <h3>1. Why Covalent Compounds Use Prefixes</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    Many simple binary covalent compounds are named using prefixes\n    that indicate how many atoms of each element are represented\n    in the molecular formula.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    A binary compound contains two different elements. For simple\n    binary covalent compounds, the prefix system helps distinguish\n    formulae that contain different numbers of atoms.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>2. Common Prefixes</h3>\n\n  <table>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Number of atoms</th>\n      <th>Prefix</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>mono-</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>di-</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>tri-</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>tetra-</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>penta-</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>hexa-</td>\n    </tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>3. Basic Naming Rules</h3>\n\n  <ol>\n    <li>Name the first element using its element name.</li>\n    <li>For the first element, the prefix mono- is usually omitted when there is only one atom.</li>\n    <li>Name the second element using its modified form, commonly ending in -ide.</li>\n    <li>Use a prefix for the number of atoms of the second element, including mono- when required.</li>\n    <li>Use the appropriate prefixes to distinguish different atom counts.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <p>\n    These rules apply to simple binary covalent compounds. Some\n    familiar compounds have established names that do not follow\n    this prefix pattern, so the prefix method should not be applied\n    indiscriminately to every covalent substance.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 1: Name CO2</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>\n  <p>C is carbon; O is oxygen.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count the atoms.</p>\n  <p>There is one carbon atom and two oxygen atoms.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the first element.</p>\n  <p>One carbon atom is named carbon; mono- is omitted for the first element.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Name the second element.</p>\n  <p>Two oxygen atoms use the prefix di-, and oxygen becomes oxide.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carbon dioxide.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name CO</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>\n  <p>C is carbon; O is oxygen.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count the atoms.</p>\n  <p>There is one carbon atom and one oxygen atom.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the first element.</p>\n  <p>Use carbon; omit mono- for the first element.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Name the second element.</p>\n  <p>One oxygen atom uses mono- with oxide, giving monoxide.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carbon monoxide.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 3: Name N2O3</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>\n  <p>N is nitrogen; O is oxygen.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count the atoms.</p>\n  <p>There are two nitrogen atoms and three oxygen atoms.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the first element.</p>\n  <p>Two nitrogen atoms use di-, giving dinitrogen.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Name the second element.</p>\n  <p>Three oxygen atoms use tri- with oxide, giving trioxide.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Dinitrogen trioxide.</p>\n\n  <h3>4. Avoiding Naming Errors</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Read the subscripts to count atoms of each element.</li>\n    <li>Do not confuse ionic naming with covalent prefix naming.</li>\n    <li>Usually omit mono- for the first element when there is one atom.</li>\n    <li>Use the modified name of the second element, such as oxide.</li>\n    <li>Check that the prefixes match the formula's subscripts.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Remember</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    Prefixes communicate atom counts in the names of many simple\n    binary covalent compounds. The name should match the molecular\n    formula, not an assumed ratio.\n  </p>\n  ",
