      "notes": "<h2>Calculating Mass from Moles</h2>\n\n  <h3>1. The relationship between mass, moles and molar mass</h3>\n\n  <p>Sometimes the amount of substance is known, but the mass is required. For example, a laboratory procedure may require 0.25 mol of a substance, and the learner must calculate how many grams to weigh.</p>\n\n  <p>The relationship is:</p>\n\n  <p><strong>m = n × M</strong></p>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>m = mass in g</li>\n    <li>n = amount of substance in mol</li>\n    <li>M = molar mass in g mol⁻¹</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p><strong>In words:</strong> mass = amount in moles × mass per mole.</p>\n\n  <h3>2. Why multiplication works</h3>\n\n  <p>If one mole of a substance has a mass of 18 g, then two moles have a mass of 2 × 18 = 36 g.</p>\n\n  <p>Likewise, half a mole has a mass of 0.5 × 18 = 9 g.</p>\n\n  <p>The amount in moles tells us how many one-mole portions we have. Multiplying that amount by the mass of each mole gives the total mass.</p>\n\n  <h3>3. Choose the correct molar mass</h3>\n\n  <p>Always identify the substance and calculate its molar mass before substituting into the equation. Do not use the molar mass of a different substance just because it appears in a previous question.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 1: Mass of 3 mol of water</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the mass of 3 mol of H₂O. Use H = 1 and O = 16.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1: Calculate molar mass.</strong></p>\n\n  <p>M(H₂O) = (2 × 1) + 16 = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 2: Write the formula.</strong></p>\n\n  <p>m = n × M</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute.</strong></p>\n\n  <p>m = 3 × 18</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate.</strong></p>\n\n  <p>m = 54 g.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 54 g of water.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 2: Mass of 0.25 mol of carbon dioxide</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the mass of 0.25 mol of CO₂. Use C = 12 and O = 16.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate M(CO₂) = 12 + (2 × 16) = 44 g mol⁻¹.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use m = n × M.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute m = 0.25 × 44.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate m = 11 g.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 11 g of CO₂.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Check:</strong> One-quarter of 44 g is 11 g, so the result is reasonable.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 3: Mass of 1.5 mol of calcium carbonate</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the mass of 1.5 mol of CaCO₃. Use Ca = 40, C = 12 and O = 16.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> M(CaCO₃) = 40 + 12 + (3 × 16) = 100 g mol⁻¹.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use m = n × M.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute m = 1.5 × 100.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate m = 150 g.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 150 g of CaCO₃.</p>\n\n  <h3>4. Checking the units</h3>\n\n  <p>Mass = mol × g mol⁻¹.</p>\n\n  <p>The mol units cancel, leaving grams. Therefore, the final answer must be a mass in grams when the molar mass is given in g mol⁻¹.</p>\n\n  <h3>5. Connecting both mass–mole formulas</h3>\n\n  <p>The two formulas describe the same relationship:</p>\n\n  <p><strong>n = m ÷ M</strong></p>\n\n  <p><strong>m = n × M</strong></p>\n\n  <p>Use division when finding moles from mass. Use multiplication when finding mass from moles.</p>\n\n  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Dividing by molar mass when the mass is required.</li>\n    <li>Using relative formula mass without the molar-mass unit.</li>\n    <li>Using the wrong chemical formula to calculate molar mass.</li>\n    <li>Reporting a mass in moles or an amount in grams.</li>\n  </ul>",
