      "notes": "\n  <h2>Universal Indicator</h2>\n\n  <h3>1. What is universal indicator?</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    Universal indicator is a mixture of indicators that produces\n    different colours across a range of pH values. It helps estimate\n    how acidic or alkaline a solution is.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    Unlike litmus, which mainly distinguishes acidic from alkaline\n    solutions, universal indicator gives a range of colours that can\n    be compared with a reference colour chart.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>2. Interpreting its colours</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Strongly acidic:</strong> typically red.</li>\n    <li><strong>Weakly acidic:</strong> typically orange or yellow.</li>\n    <li><strong>Near neutral:</strong> typically green around pH 7.</li>\n    <li><strong>Alkaline:</strong> typically blue to purple as pH increases.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>\n    The exact colour depends on the indicator formulation and the\n    reference chart. Always compare the result with the chart supplied\n    for the indicator being used.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>\n\n  <p><strong>Example 1: A red universal-indicator result</strong></p>\n\n  <p>\n    Universal indicator turns red in a sample. What does the colour\n    suggest?\n  </p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare red with the universal-indicator chart.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Red corresponds to a strongly acidic region.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is strongly acidic, subject to the chart used.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Example 2: A green result</strong></p>\n\n  <p>\n    Universal indicator turns green. What pH region does this usually\n    suggest?\n  </p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Green is the typical neutral colour.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Neutral aqueous solutions are represented around pH 7 on the usual school-level chart.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution is approximately neutral, around pH 7.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Example 3: Comparing two solutions</strong></p>\n\n  <p>\n    Sample A produces a yellow colour, while Sample B produces a blue\n    colour. Which sample is more alkaline?\n  </p>\n\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Yellow typically indicates an acidic region.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Blue typically indicates an alkaline region.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sample B is more alkaline.</p>\n\n  <h3>4. Limitations</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    Universal indicator estimates pH by colour comparison. It does not\n    normally give the precision of a properly calibrated pH meter.\n    A contaminated sample or poor lighting can make the colour harder\n    to interpret.\n  </p>\n  ",
