      "notes": "\n  <h2>Alkanes</h2>\n\n  <h3>1. Definition</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Alkanes</b> are saturated open-chain hydrocarbons containing\n    only single bonds between carbon atoms.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    Because their carbon-carbon bonds are single bonds, alkanes contain\n    the maximum number of hydrogen atoms possible for an open-chain\n    hydrocarbon with that number of carbon atoms.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>2. Names of the First Five Alkanes</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Methane — CH<sub>4</sub></li>\n    <li>Ethane — C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub></li>\n    <li>Propane — C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub></li>\n    <li>Butane — C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub></li>\n    <li>Pentane — C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>12</sub></li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>3. Recognising an Alkane</h3>\n\n  <p>\n    To identify an alkane, check that the substance is a hydrocarbon\n    and that its carbon framework is an open chain containing only\n    single carbon-carbon bonds.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    The general formula for open-chain alkanes is\n    C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>, where n is the number of carbon atoms.\n    The formula is explored in detail on the dedicated formula screen.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify an Alkane</h3>\n\n  <p><b>Question:</b> Ethane contains two carbon atoms joined by a single bond. Which hydrocarbon family does it belong to?</p>\n  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ethane contains carbon and hydrogen only.</p>\n  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its carbon atoms are joined by a single bond.</p>\n  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethane belongs to the alkane family.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name C3H8</h3>\n\n  <p><b>Question:</b> Name the alkane containing three carbon atoms.</p>\n  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The prefix for three carbon atoms is prop-.</p>\n  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The suffix for an alkane is -ane.</p>\n  <p><b>Answer:</b> Propane, C3H8.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked Example 3: Compare Ethane and Ethene</h3>\n\n  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why ethane is an alkane but ethene is an alkene.</p>\n  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ethane has a single C–C bond.</p>\n  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Ethene has a C=C double bond.</p>\n  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethane is saturated and belongs to the alkanes; ethene is unsaturated and belongs to the alkenes.</p>\n  ",
