      "notes": "<h2>Naming Alkenes</h2>\n\n  <h3>1. What is an alkene?</h3>\n  <p>An alkene is an unsaturated hydrocarbon containing a carbon-carbon double bond. In naming an alkene, the parent chain must include the double bond.</p>\n\n  <p>The ending <strong>-ene</strong> indicates a carbon-carbon double bond. The position number identifies the first carbon of that double bond in the numbered parent chain.</p>\n\n  <h3>2. Naming procedure</h3>\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the longest continuous carbon chain that contains the double bond.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number the chain from the end nearest the double bond.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the position where the double bond begins.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Use the carbon prefix and the ending -ene.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked example 1: CH₂=CH₂</h3>\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> There are two carbon atoms, so use eth-.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There is a carbon-carbon double bond, so use -ene.</p>\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethene. A position number is unnecessary because only one position is possible.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked example 2: CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃</h3>\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The chain has four carbon atoms, so use but-.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number from the end nearest the double bond.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The double bond begins at carbon 1.</p>\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-1-ene.</p>\n\n  <h3>Worked example 3: CH₃CH=CHCH₃</h3>\n  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The chain contains four carbon atoms.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The double bond lies between carbons 2 and 3.</p>\n  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Numbering from either end gives locant 2.</p>\n  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-2-ene.</p>\n\n  <h3>Important check</h3>\n  <p>The position number refers to the first carbon involved in the double bond. A four-carbon chain with a double bond beginning at carbon 1 is but-1-ene; one beginning at carbon 2 is but-2-ene.</p>",
